NAME
    boot - how TOS starts, and the boot spectrum

DESCRIPTION
    Boot is two buckets: B1, the EEPROM BIOS (tiny, ~4 KiB), and B2, the main
    init.lua + kernel. B1 finds the boot disk, runs power-on self-tests, and
    hands off to B2, which reads /etc/boot.cfg and brings up the system.

THE BOOT SPECTRUM (/etc/boot.cfg)
    profile     WHAT LOADS: minimal | normal (default) | full | diagnostic.
    verbosity   WHAT IT SAYS (a "muter", not behavior): silent | splash | text
                (default) | verbose.
    advanced    Per-feature on/off/auto overrides (swap, power, net, theme,
                compat, audio, integrityCheck).
    cpuTier     Confirm/correct the detected CPU tier (auto | 1 | 2 | 3).
    showConfig  Toggle the System Configuration POST screen.

    A missing or corrupt boot.cfg is safe: TOS falls back to the 'normal'
    profile, i.e. exactly how it has always booted.

THE POST SCREEN AND BOOT SETTINGS
    Each non-silent boot briefly shows the System Configuration screen (installed
    hardware with tiers). While it shows "Press DEL for Boot Settings...", press
    DELETE to open the visual editor. Missed it? Run 'bootsettings' in the shell.

FALLBACK & ONE-TIME BOOT
    If the boot drive is gone or changed, the BIOS will NOT silently adopt a new
    disk. It asks: Y updates the EEPROM permanently, Shift+Enter boots once
    without touching it (a "one-time boot"), N/timeout halts.

SEE ALSO
